## Part 2: Three grep searches on `app.log`

### Search 1: show ERROR lines with line numbers

```bash
grep -n "ERROR" app.log
```

Expected: 4 lines (lines 5, 8, 11, 15).

### Search 2: count "failed", ignoring case

```bash
grep -ic "failed" app.log
```

Expected: 4. `-i` ignores case, so it matches both "Failed" and "failed". `-c` prints a count instead of the lines.

### Search 3: show everything that is NOT an INFO line

```bash
grep -v "INFO" app.log
```

Expected: 7 lines (every WARN and ERROR entry). `-v` inverts the match.

